["```java
// This is a simplified example and does not include all features of a production-ready task queue system.

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class TaskQueue {

    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10); // Adjust thread pool size as needed
    private final BlockingQueue<Runnable> taskQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

    public void enqueueTask(Runnable task) {
        try {
            taskQueue.put(task);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    public void start() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) { // Start worker threads
            executor.execute(new Worker());
        }
    }

    private class Worker implements Runnable {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            while (true) {
                try {
                    Runnable task = taskQueue.take();
                    task.run(); // Execute the task
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            }
        }
    }

}
```



This code provides a basic framework for a task queue system with:

* **`enqueueTask()`**:  Adds a Runnable task to the queue.
* **`start()`**: Starts worker threads that process tasks from the queue.
* **`Worker` class**: Each worker thread continuously takes tasks from the `taskQueue` and executes them.



