// This is a simplified example and does not encompass the full complexity 
// of a production-ready event bus system.

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class AsynchronousEventBus {

    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Queue<Event>> subscribers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void subscribe(String topic, EventSubscriber subscriber) {
        subscribers.computeIfAbsent(topic, k -> new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>()).add(subscriber);
    }

    public void publish(String topic, Event event) {
        subscribers.getOrDefault(topic, new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>()).forEach(subscriber -> {
            // Asynchronous processing of events:
            new Thread(() -> subscriber.handleEvent(event)).start();
        });
    }

    // ... (Implement other functionalities like security, error handling, etc.)

}

interface EventSubscriber {
    void handleEvent(Event event);
}

class Event {
    // Event data fields
}
```



**Explanation:**

This code provides a basic structure for an asynchronous event bus.  

* **`ConcurrentHashMap<String, Queue<EventSubscriber>>`**: This map stores subscribers grouped by topic. 
* **`subscribe(String topic, EventSubscriber subscriber)`**: Registers a subscriber to a specific topic.
* **`publish(String topic, Event event)`**: Publishes an event to all subscribers of the given topic.

